<!-- BRIEF -->
<section class="case-section">
  <h2>The brief.</h2>
  <div class="copy">
    <p>Reportage Properties is a UAE developer with a growing book of projects and master-planned communities. Their material — renders, brochures, fact sheets, floor plans, virtual tours, location links, construction photos — was scattered across a website, a Dropbox, and whatever a sales agent had saved on their phone. A buyer following a project had no single place to watch it take shape.</p>
    <p>The ask: a native app for iOS and Android, fully bilingual in Arabic and English, with an admin web behind it so the marketing team could add a project, post an offer, or push an update without a developer.</p>
    <div class="pull-quote">"Every project we have, everything we know about it, current — on the buyer's phone."</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- IDEA -->
<section class="case-section">
  <h2>The idea.</h2>
  <div class="copy">
    <p>Structure the whole app around the two things Reportage actually sells: <strong>projects</strong> and the <strong>communities</strong> they sit in. Everything else — search, offers, the media center, construction updates, announcements — is a different way into the same catalogue.</p>
    <p>The app opens by asking for a language, so Arabic isn't a settings-screen afterthought — the entire layout mirrors. A skippable offer takes the first screen, then a home of live renders, a search that speaks the buyer's language (community, unit type, bedrooms, budget), and a chat button one tap away on every screen.</p>
  </div>
</section>

